========================================================
23. CHECK EKS POD CAPACITY
========================================================

CHECK MAXIMUM PODS:

kubectl get node <NODE-NAME> \
-o jsonpath='{.status.capacity.pods}{"\n"}'


CHECK CURRENT POD COUNT:

kubectl get pods -A \
--field-selector spec.nodeName=<NODE-NAME> \
--no-headers | wc -l


CHECK NODE DETAILS:

kubectl describe node <NODE-NAME>


LOOK FOR:

Capacity:
  pods: 29

Allocatable:
  pods: 29


IMPORTANT:

CPU and Memory can be available while the node
still cannot schedule another Pod.

Example:

CPU       = Available
Memory    = Available
Pods      = 29/29 FULL


RESULT:

New Pod = Pending


REASON:

Too many pods


========================================================
24. CHECK PENDING POD
========================================================

kubectl describe pod <POD-NAME>


LOOK AT:

Events:

FailedScheduling


EXAMPLE:

0/1 nodes are available:
1 Too many pods.


